===============================================================================
DB2 interactive Call Level Interface (CLI) environment - execsql mode
===============================================================================

You can use the DB2 interactive CLI environment in execsql mode to perform the
following task (s):

  - Execute the set SQL statements available in the text file.
  - Prepare the set SQL statements available in the text file.
  - Capture the SQL statements in the PDQXML file.

Command syntax

  db2cli execsql [ -execute | -prepareonly ]
      { [-dsn <dsn_name> [-user <username> [ -passwd <passwd>] ] ] |
      [-connstring <connection-string>] }
      [-inputsql <sqlfile>]
      [-outfile <outputfile>]
      [-statementdelimiter <delimiter>]
      [-commentstart  <commentindicator>]
      [-cursorhold  <holdability>]
      [-cursorconcurrency <concurrency>]
      [-cursortypes <type>]
      [-help]

Command parameters

  execsql

      Execute or prepare the SQL statements given. Can also capture the SQLs in
      the PDQXML file when capturemode is enabled in the configuration files.

  -execute

      This is default mode of execsql. All the SQL will be executed and the
      results (Success or error) will be displayed.

  -prepareonly

      All the SQL will just be prepared. This is mainly for the user who wants
      to avoid actual execution of the SQLs at the server end and to check if
      the syntax of the SQL is correct without actually executing the SQL.

  -dsn <dsn_name>

      Name of the data source.

  -user <username>

      User name for connecting to the data source.

  -passwd <passwd>

      password for the specified username.

  -connstring <connection-string>

      CLI Connection string.

  -inputsql <sqlfile>

      Option to provide the input SQL file. This input SQL file will have SQL
      separated by the delimiters.

  -outfile <outputfile>

      Option to specify the file to store the output results.

  -statementdelimiter <delimiter>

      Option to specify the delimiter used in the input SQL file. The default
      delimiter is CRLF (Carriage Return/Line Feed)

  -commentstart  <commentindicator>

      Option to specify the character combination that appears at the beginning
      of a line to indicate a single line comment. The default is two dashes
      (--).

  -cursorhold  <holdability>

      Option to specify the cursor holdability for all statements. The
      holdability is one of the following values:

      yes : The cursors are not destroyed when the transaction is committed.
      no  : The cursors are destroyed when the transaction is committed.

  -cursorconcurrency <concurrency>

      Option to specify the cursor concurrency for all the statements. The
      concurrency is one of the following values:

      readonly : Sets the cursor concurrency to be read only. This is the
      default.

      lock     : Cursor uses the lowest level of locking sufficient to ensure
      that the row can be updated.

      values   : Cursor uses optimistic concurrency control, comparing values

  -cursortypes <type>

      Specifies which cursor types are permitted. The cursortypes is one of the
      following values:

      forwardonly : The cursor only scrolls forward. This is the default.

      static      : The data in the result set is static.

      keyset      : Unlike a static scrollable cursor, a keyset-driven
      scrollable cursor can detect and make changes to the underlying data.

      dynamic     : A dynamic scrollable cursor detects all changes (inserts,
      deletes and updates) to the result set and make insertions, deletions and
      updates to result set.

  -help

      Display db2cli execsql command help and usage information.